WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.468
But we're going to start with Democrats

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:03.370
putting a great big exclamation

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.338
point on the 2022 midterm season

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:06.773
and projecting incumbent Senator

00:00:06.773 --> 00:00:09.776
Raphael Warnock has defeated Trump picked

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:11.878
Republican challenger Herschel Walker.

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:13.446
In Georgia's runoff election.

00:00:13.446 --> 00:00:14.314
President Joe Biden

00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:17.150
calling Warnock to congratulate him,

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:18.385
tweeting this as well.

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:18.718
Tonight,

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:19.452
Georgia voters

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:22.055
stood up for democracy, for our democracy

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:22.822
and reject it.

00:00:22.822 --> 00:00:24.357
Ultra MAGA ism

00:00:24.357 --> 00:00:24.691
or not

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.325
got emotional

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:27.861
as he thanked his supporters

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:30.296
My roots,

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:31.965
like the roots of those oak trees,

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:34.801
go deep down into the soil of Savannah

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:35.602
and Waycross

00:00:35.602 --> 00:00:38.638
and Scriven County in Berks County.

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.840
I am Georgia

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:44.010
Well, Herschel Walker

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.046
was quick to concede, sort of

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:47.580
you have to really listen to the language

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:48.148
in what he said.

00:00:48.148 --> 00:00:49.783
He didn't really talk about his opponent.

00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:51.384
Just think to supporters

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:52.652
vowing to never stop fighting

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:53.720
for the people of Georgia.

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:57.857
There's no excuses in life.

00:00:57.857 --> 00:00:59.659
And I'm not going to make an excuse now

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:02.228
because we put up one heck of a fight.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.830
And I think that's what

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.665
that's what we got to do

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:07.834
because this is much bigger.

00:01:07.834 --> 00:01:10.770
This is much bigger than Herschel Walker.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.438
So, look,

00:01:11.438 --> 00:01:12.806
you guys don't want to see me

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:13.940
this morning.

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:15.175
This is who you want to see.

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:17.143
John Berman, the guy the guy of the hour.

00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:18.178
The man of the hour.

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:19.946
Besides Raphael Warnock,

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:21.648
I should say at the magic wall,

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:22.682
everyone wants to know what happened.

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:23.450
You've got the numbers.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.218
You have the evidence. So take it away.

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:26.019
Well, here's the margin.

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.387
First of all, Raphael Warnock

00:01:27.387 --> 00:01:30.957
with a 95,000 vote margin, about 2.8%.

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.193
Compare that to just one month ago.

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:33.960
The general election

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.495
was only one month ago.

00:01:35.495 --> 00:01:35.895
You can see

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:39.632
Warnock led by 0.9% and 37,000 votes.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:41.968
So he's grown that lead.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.570
Where has he done it?

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.139
Let's look at where Warnock overperformed

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:47.340
one month ago.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.842
Almost everywhere, right?

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:51.511
Almost every county here is lit up.

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:52.912
So let's look at it a different way.

00:01:52.912 --> 00:01:54.814
Where did Herschel Walker

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:56.950
underperform in? This is telling.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:57.917
Look at this.

00:01:57.917 --> 00:01:58.318
Because what

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:00.153
all these areas have in common.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:01.955
You have the Atlanta area,

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:03.723
the urban and suburban area.

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:05.525
They're right around Columbus,

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:06.960
right around Augusta,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.461
right around Savannah.

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:10.830
While these areas have in common, it's

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.333
where the large population centers are.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.001
The bigger the circle,

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:16.703
the more the population,

00:02:16.703 --> 00:02:18.605
all the big circles really

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:19.739
in those areas

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:22.275
where Herschel Walker underperformed.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:23.576
And what's interesting

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:25.545
and I think as you talk to Republicans

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:26.679
over the course of the morning,

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:27.714
what you'll find,

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:28.314
the areas

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:29.749
they're most concerned about,

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.318
the suburban areas around Atlanta.

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:34.754
Take Gwinnett County, for instance.

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:35.488
You can see

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.524
Raphael Warnock won Gwinnett County.

00:02:37.524 --> 00:02:40.126
What's the math there by about plus 25.

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:41.528
Let's do D-plus.

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:44.130
25 in Gwinnett County.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.832
That's a big margin,

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:46.833
especially when you're talking

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:49.035
about hundreds of thousands of votes

00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.404
just two years ago.

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.406
That margin was D-plus.

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:55.909
21. Okay.

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:58.178
And the presidential election,

00:02:58.178 --> 00:02:59.012
which is right around

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:02.982
that time it was D plus 18.

00:03:03.383 --> 00:03:05.351
Go back to the presidential election

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.419
in 2016.

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:07.754
Hillary Clinton.

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:10.323
It's D-plus See a trend there.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:10.990
I do.

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:11.691
It's growing

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:12.759
or getting smaller depending

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:14.761
how you look at it in 2012.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:18.164
Mitt Romney won Gwinnett County

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:19.666
ten years ago.

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:20.967
You went from Republicans

00:03:20.967 --> 00:03:21.935
winning that county

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:25.672
by what nine points almost to now

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:27.807
you have a Democrat winning it

00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:29.676
by 25 points.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:31.244
That's a remarkable turnaround.

00:03:31.244 --> 00:03:34.047
And again these are the suburban areas.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:35.381
And you lived there for a time, Don.

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:38.051
You know, it's not just urban Atlanta.

00:03:38.051 --> 00:03:38.518
It's East

00:03:38.518 --> 00:03:41.387
growing suburban areas that are booming

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:43.856
where Democrats are gaining a population

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.425
and gaining in popularity areas

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:47.393
that are more diverse. Yeah, absolutely.

00:03:47.393 --> 00:03:48.294
I mean, but listen,

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:49.195
that's why, you know,

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:49.629
people say, well,

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:51.331
Georgia is a pink state,

00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:52.265
a purple or whatever it is.

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:53.766
But I mean, look at all the red there.

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:54.534
I still consider

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:55.501
Georgia to be a red state.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.904
That's just my personal opinion.

00:03:57.904 --> 00:03:58.838
The cities

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:00.907
and the surrounding suburbs, blue.

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:01.341
But I mean,

00:04:01.341 --> 00:04:02.375
if you look at the vast

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:04.510
majority, at least land wise, area wise,

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:06.412
it's still a pretty red state.

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:08.381
It is. But geography doesn't vote.

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:09.182
Right? Right. Right.

00:04:09.182 --> 00:04:09.882
So you're looking at all

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:10.550
the red counties.

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:11.384
I'll put that up again

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:13.486
where Walker outperformed Walker.

00:04:13.486 --> 00:04:14.754
Geography votes, though,

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:15.655
in national elections.

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:16.356
Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:17.690
Well, with the Electoral College

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:18.658
it has a little bit of advantage.

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:19.359
But you look here.

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:20.493
Look, Herschel Walker

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:22.562
did better in Dade County

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:24.264
than he did one month ago.

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:25.999
But you can see you're only talking about

00:04:25.999 --> 00:04:27.600
5000 votes. Yes.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:28.268
He did better.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:29.602
He got more percent,

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:30.970
two more percent than he did

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:31.671
one month ago.

00:04:31.671 --> 00:04:33.573
But you're dealing with 5000 votes.

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:35.074
And you compare that to Don,

00:04:35.074 --> 00:04:36.709
you compare it to Fulton County.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:37.410
Again,

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.979
you're talking about several hundred

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:41.581
thousand votes.

00:04:41.581 --> 00:04:43.049
DeKalb County, several

00:04:43.049 --> 00:04:44.851
hundred thousand votes.

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:46.052
It's where the people live

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:46.653
right now,

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:48.254
and particularly in suburban areas

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:49.989
that used to be Republican strongholds.

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:51.524
Cobb County right here,

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:52.125
this is Newt

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:53.993
Gingrich lived here,

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:55.962
and now it's voting for Democrats by now

00:04:55.962 --> 00:04:56.429
points.

00:04:56.429 --> 00:04:56.963
Wow.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.065
Now, that is a fun fact, so to speak.

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:00.400
John Berman, always a pleasure.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:00.967
Thank you, sir.

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:02.335
Gentlemen, great seeing you said it.

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:04.337
Thanks, Don and John.

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:04.937
And as you know,

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:06.439
we're looking at those numbers.

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:06.706
Let's

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:09.042
bring in the Atlanta Journal-Constitution

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:10.710
political reporter Patricia Murphy

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.245
to talk about this.

00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:12.812
One of the things

00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:13.813
we were watching last night

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:15.548
were how these candidates fared in

00:05:15.548 --> 00:05:17.583
November versus how they did last night.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:18.918
As John was just highlighting

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:20.820
there, we're not doing much better

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.222
in those metro Atlanta areas. We're not.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:24.324
Walker did okay

00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:26.159
in some of the ruby red areas.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:27.360
The rural parts of the state.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.095
But what were your biggest takeaways

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:31.164
of what changed from just a month ago

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:32.432
to this runoff election?

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.267
So as John Berman

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.002
laid out really, really well,

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:36.803
Herschel Walker

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:38.805
had one job to do last night,

00:05:38.805 --> 00:05:40.039
and that was to expand

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:41.908
his lead in those rural areas,

00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:43.810
to really start to run up his numbers

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.611
and to catch up with where Brian Kemp

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:47.447
had been just a month ago.

00:05:47.447 --> 00:05:48.614
Herschel Walker didn't do that.

00:05:48.614 --> 00:05:49.349
He even shrank

00:05:49.349 --> 00:05:51.284
in some of those rural areas.

00:05:51.284 --> 00:05:53.286
He grew his numbers in some cases,

00:05:53.286 --> 00:05:54.654
but only by one or 2%.

00:05:54.654 --> 00:05:57.090
He needed to grow that by five or 6%.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.025
And so we didn't see him do that.

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:01.060
And at the same time, Raphael Warnock

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:01.828
did expand

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:02.261
his lead

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:03.096
in the big metro

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:05.665
areas around Savannah, Augusta, Columbus,

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:06.933
Atlanta, of course.

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:07.934
And so

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:09.001
they both

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:10.970
were dealing with a change in enthusiasm.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:11.671
It really felt

00:06:11.671 --> 00:06:12.572
like Herschel

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:14.874
Walker's enthusiasm for his candidacy

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:17.543
really fell off almost a cliff.

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:18.811
You could feel it in the state

00:06:18.811 --> 00:06:20.346
and you could feel Raphael Warnock

00:06:20.346 --> 00:06:22.115
and the Democrats say to themselves,

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:22.849
okay,

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:25.118
this this party just lost

00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:27.320
eight statewide elections in November.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.889
We have one more chance to win statewide.

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:31.391
And that was Raphael Warnock.

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:33.726
He also ran a totally different campaign

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:34.761
from Herschel Walker

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:36.028
reached out to those moderate

00:06:36.028 --> 00:06:36.996
and independent voters.

00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:38.164
And he was everywhere,

00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:39.499
all across the state

00:06:39.499 --> 00:06:40.533
throughout the runoff.

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:41.701
And Herschel Walker

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:42.301
really kept

00:06:42.301 --> 00:06:43.469
a very limited schedule,

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:45.004
didn't get back out to the areas

00:06:45.004 --> 00:06:46.139
he hit before.

00:06:46.139 --> 00:06:47.340
And so you just saw that

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.541
in the numbers last night.

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:49.208
And he actually took

00:06:49.208 --> 00:06:50.843
five days off the campaign trail.

00:06:50.843 --> 00:06:53.012
And that's been a source of frustration

00:06:53.012 --> 00:06:53.913
that I've heard from

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:56.249
from some people in Walker's campaign.

00:06:56.249 --> 00:06:58.384
He came out the last night and conceded

00:06:58.384 --> 00:07:00.720
and his campaign made clear, yes,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.555
that was a concession speech.

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:02.922
Yes.

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:03.990
Because in his speech

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.125
he didn't say I called Raphael Warnock.

00:07:06.125 --> 00:07:06.959
He didn't say that.

00:07:06.959 --> 00:07:09.295
He didn't say I officially concede,

00:07:09.295 --> 00:07:11.030
but he wasn't saying that he won.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:13.166
He did call himself and his supporters

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:14.634
winners that really just

00:07:14.634 --> 00:07:16.803
we we won because we tried

00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:18.271
and we did our best.

00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:19.572
But afterward, his staff did

00:07:19.572 --> 00:07:21.574
tell reporters that was a concession.

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:23.309
This is there's going to be no attempt

00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:24.744
to challenge these results.

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:25.678
And I think it's something

00:07:25.678 --> 00:07:26.412
that the state

00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:28.648
really needed to see from Republicans.

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:30.450
This is the first time

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:31.784
that we've had a Democrat win

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:33.753
statewide since the last elections.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:35.822
And Herschel Walker

00:07:35.822 --> 00:07:37.890
coming out and conceding

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.426
and sort of stepping off the stage.

00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:41.627
I think that's something

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:42.495
that the state needed to see.

00:07:42.495 --> 00:07:43.663
And I think that's something that he did

00:07:43.663 --> 00:07:44.263
well last night.

00:07:44.263 --> 00:07:45.832
And, you know, Georgia so well,

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:46.632
what John and

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:48.000
I were just talking about there is

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.302
what does this mean for the state?

00:07:49.302 --> 00:07:50.736
Does it mean it's more of a purple state?

00:07:50.736 --> 00:07:51.437
But as you noted,

00:07:51.437 --> 00:07:55.274
you Republicans won every every election

00:07:55.274 --> 00:07:56.709
on the ticket statewide

00:07:56.709 --> 00:07:58.778
except for Herschel Walker's seat.

00:07:58.778 --> 00:08:00.213
And so what's your sense?

00:08:00.213 --> 00:08:00.513
Was it

00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:01.481
was it a more about

00:08:01.481 --> 00:08:03.182
a reflection of the state or was it

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:04.484
just candidate quality?

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:05.284
I think it's both.

00:08:05.284 --> 00:08:06.452
I mean, you can't ignore

00:08:06.452 --> 00:08:08.521
the candidate quality of Herschel Walker

00:08:08.554 --> 00:08:09.722
during the runoff period.

00:08:09.722 --> 00:08:10.623
He also had

00:08:10.623 --> 00:08:12.625
his major gaffe about vampires

00:08:12.625 --> 00:08:13.926
versus werewolves.

00:08:13.926 --> 00:08:14.527
There were more

00:08:14.527 --> 00:08:16.596
accusations of abuse against him

00:08:16.829 --> 00:08:17.763
that came out as well.

00:08:17.763 --> 00:08:20.233
So he had a really bad runoff campaign

00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:22.969
after a really tough general election.

00:08:22.969 --> 00:08:24.270
Campaign as well for him,

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.072
just not a great candidate.

00:08:26.072 --> 00:08:28.207
However, what we see in Georgia right now

00:08:28.207 --> 00:08:29.742
is that this is a true

00:08:29.742 --> 00:08:30.843
battleground state.

00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:33.546
A candidate from either party can win,

00:08:33.546 --> 00:08:35.081
and that's a place that Georgia hasn't

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:37.116
been in decades. It has.

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:39.151
I can't remember the last time

00:08:39.151 --> 00:08:39.952
somebody could say, I'm

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:40.786
going to run statewide.

00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:42.588
I'm a Democrat, I'm a Republican,

00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:43.489
and there are reasons

00:08:43.489 --> 00:08:44.590
for those national parties

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:45.791
to get behind them

00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:46.626
because they've all got

00:08:46.626 --> 00:08:48.094
a really good shot at winning this state.

00:08:48.094 --> 00:08:49.662
It's winnable right now for both parties.

